@node Naming system
@section Naming system

In SmallTalk, variable names use @code{case-sensitive} naming (conventionally @code{camelCase} for variables and @code{UpperCamelCase} for classes).
All keywords are case-sensitive and written in lowercase.

@table @asis
@item Keywords: self, super, true, false, nil, thisContext, ifTrue:, ifFalse:, ifTrue:ifFalse:, whileTrue:, whileFalse:, timesRepeat:, to:do:, to:by:do:
@item Operators: +, -, *, /, //, \\, =, ~=, >, <, >=, <=, and, or, not
@item String delimiters: Single quotes (@code{'...'}) for strings, double quotes (@code{"..."}) for comments
@item Comments: Double quotes (@code{"..."}) to end of line
@end table

@node Basic structure
@section Basic structure for a stand-alone routine or program

A SmallTalk program consists of class definitions and method definitions, and is executed by sending messages to objects.

@example
! Initialize game !
| score livesLeft currentRoom |
score := 0.
livesLeft := 3.
currentRoom := 1.

GameState new
    initializeGame;
    [
        gameOver not
    ] whileTrue: [
        self processInput.
        self updateGame.
        self renderDisplay.
    ].

! Define GameState class !
Object subclass: #GameState
    instanceVariableNames: 'score livesLeft currentRoom gameOver'
    classVariableNames: ''
    poolDictionaries: ''
    category: 'Phantasia-Game' !

! Methods !
GameState>>initializeGame
    score := 0.
    livesLeft := 3.
    currentRoom := 1.
    gameOver := false.

GameState>>processInput
    "Simulate input handling"
    (self keyPressed: #rightArrow) ifTrue: [
        currentRoom := currentRoom + 10.
    ].

GameState>>updateGame
    "Game logic updates".

GameState>>renderDisplay
    "Rendering code".
@end example

@node Basic structure object
@section Basic structure for an object method

SmallTalk is purely object-oriented; all computation is done by sending messages to objects.

@example
! Define Player class !
Object subclass: #Player
    instanceVariableNames: 'x y sprite'
    classVariableNames: ''
    poolDictionaries: ''
    category: 'Phantasia-Game' !

Player>>initialize
    super initialize.
    x := 0.
    y := 0.
    sprite := 0.

Player>>moveDX:dx dy:dy
    x := x + dx.
    y := y + dy.
! End !

! Create instance !
| player |
player := Player new.
player initialize.
player moveDX: 5 dy: 10.
@end example

@node Calling routines
@section Calling a routine (nullary)

To send a message that takes no arguments:

@example
initializeGraphics.
clearScreen.
playStartupMusic.
someObject someMessage.
@end example

@node Calling routines value
@section Calling a routine with a value (in accumulator)

To send a message that returns a single value:

@example
| score bonusFactor volume |
score := someObject calculateBonus: level withTimeLeft: timeLeft.
bonusFactor := someObject getInputFactor.
volume := someObject getMasterVolume.
someObject someMessage.
@end example

@node Calling routines giving
@section Calling a routine giving a value (in accumulator)

Not applicable in SmallTalk's standard calling convention.

@node Calling routines values
@section Calling a routine with values in and out (in accumulator)

To send a message with parameters:

@example
someObject drawSprite: spriteId atX: xPos andY: yPos.
someObject playSound: soundId withVolume: volume andLoopFlag: loopFlag.
someObject checkCollision: obj1 with: obj2 puttingResultIn: resultVar.
someObject someMessageWithParam1: param1 andParam2: param2.
@end example

@node Calling library
@section Calling a library routine (all 4 styles)

SmallTalk accesses library routines through standard message sends:

@example
someObject loadLevel: levelNumber.          @r{Direct call}
someObject farProc: libraryAddress.         @r{Far call}
| result |
result := someObject libraryFunction: input. @r{Library function}
someObject someObject: param1 with: param2.   @r{Object method call}
@end example

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: OBJECT messageName
@item Far: OBJECT farProc: address
@item Library: OBJECT libraryFunction: input
@item Object: OBJECT someMessage:param1 with:param2
@end table

@node Calling far bank
@section Calling a far bank Service (all 4 styles)

Far procedures in SmallTalk are called using external interfaces or through
the foreign function interface (if available):

@example
! Assume we have a C function farLoadLibrary at address 0x8000 !
| farLoadLibrary |
farLoadLibrary := ExternalFunction
    returnType: #void
    parameterTypes: #( #unsignedInt )
    module: 'farproc'
    function: #farLoadLibrary.

farLoadLibrary value: 16r8000.   @r{Call routine in bank 1}
@end example

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: Not used for far calls
@item Far: External function call
@item Library: Not applicable
@item Object: Not applicable
@end table

@node Invoking object
@section Invoking a method on an object (all 4 styles)

Object method invocation in SmallTalk is done by sending messages:

@example
player moveDX: 5 dy: 10.
score-display update: score.
game-state isGameOver.
@end example

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: Setting object properties via setter messages
@item Far: Not applicable for methods (objects typically in same memory)
@item Library: Method may call library routines internally
@item Object: METHOD invocation on object instance
@end table

@node Assignment
@section Assigning to a variable

Variables are assigned using the assignment operator (:=):

@example
| score livesLeft counter message |
score := 0.                           @r{Explicit assignment}
livesLeft := 3.                       @r{Implicit assignment}
counter := counter + 1.               @r{Increment}
livesLeft := livesLeft - 1.           @r{Decrement}
score := score + 10.                  @r{Arithmetic assignment}
message := 'Hello, World!'.           @r{String assignment}
@end example

@node Assignment object
@section Assigning to an object slot

Object slots (instance variables) are assigned via setter messages or direct access (if allowed):

@example
player x: 160.                          @r{Set object's x coordinate}
player y: 100.                          @r{Set object's y coordinate}
player sprite: 5.                       @r{Set sprite number}
player visible: true.                   @r{Set visibility flag}
@end example

@node Assignment array
@section Assigning to an array member with a subscript

SmallTalk uses Array and other collections. Elements are assigned using at:put: or similar:

@example
| scoreList initialsList board |
scoreList := Array new: 10.
initialsList := Array new: 10.
board := Array new: 8.
1 to: 8 do: [ :i | board at: i put: (Array new: 8) ].

scoreList at: 1 put: 100.
initialsList at: 1 put: 'ALI'.
(board at: 3) at: 4 put: 1.
@end example

@node Arithmetic
@section Basic arithmetic: addition and subtraction

Basic arithmetic uses the + and - operators:

@example
score := score + 10.                  @r{Addition}
livesLeft := livesLeft - 1.           @r{Subtraction}
| total |
total := width + height.              @r{Add two variables}
| finalPrice |
finalPrice := price - discount.       @r{Subtraction}
| delta |
delta := newValue - oldValue.         @r{Subtraction}
@end example

@node Arithmetic bit
@section Basic arithmetic: bit-shifting for mutiplication or division

Bit-shifting for multiplication/division by powers of two is not directly supported in standard SmallTalk.
Use arithmetic shift or external libraries:

@example
| result |
result := value bitShift: 2.          @r{Left shift 2 bits (multiply by 4)}
result := value bitShift: -1.         @r{Right shift 1 bit (divide by 2)}
result := value * 4.                  @r{Multiply by 4}
result := value / 2.                  @r{Divide by 2}
result := value * 8.                  @r{Multiply by 8}
result := value / 16.                 @r{Divide by 16}
@end example

@node Complex arithmetic
@section Complex arithmetic: multiple expressions

Complex arithmetic expressions follow standard precedence rules:

@example
| total average distance |
total := (baseSalary * 12) + bonus - deductions.
average := (sum1 + sum2 + sum3) / 3.0.
distance := ((x2 - x1) squared + (y2 - y1) squared) sqrt.
@end example

@node Boolean
@section Boolean expressions: equality and inequalities

Boolean expressions use logical operators and comparison operators:

@example
(score > 1000) ifTrue: [ ... ].              @r{Greater than}
(score < 50) ifTrue: [ ... ].                @r{Less than}
(score = 100) ifTrue: [ ... ].               @r{Equality}
(score ~= 0) ifTrue: [ ... ].                @r{Inequality}
((x >= 0) and: [ x < MAX_X ]) ifTrue: [ ... ] @r{Combined conditions}
((button = BUTTON_A) or: [ button = BUTTON_B ]) ifTrue: [ ... ] @r{Alternative conditions}
@end example

@node If then else
@section Using if/then/else structures and examine/cond structures

Conditional execution uses ifTrue:/ifFalse: and ifTrue:ifFalse: messages:

@example
(temperature > THOT)
    ifTrue: [ status := 'HOT' ]
    ifFalse: [
        (temperature > TCOLD)
            ifTrue: [ status := 'WARM' ]
            ifFalse: [ status := 'COLD' ]
    ].

(answer = 'Y') or: [ answer = 'y' ] ifTrue: [ response := 'YES' ] ifFalse: [
    (answer = 'N') or: [ answer = 'n' ]
        ifTrue: [ response := 'NO' ]
        ifFalse: [ response := 'INVALID' ]
].
@end example

SmallTalk does not have an EXAMINE/COND structure; the ifTrue:/ifFalse: messages serve
a similar purpose for conditional execution, and nested conditionals can handle multi-way branching.

@node Performing
@section Performing loops

Loops are implemented using whileTrue:, whileFalse:, timesRepeat:, and to:do: messages:

@example
! whileTrue: loop equivalent (pre-test) !
| i |
i := 1.
[ i <= 10 ] whileTrue: [
    'Count: ', i asString printNl.
    i := i + 1.
].

! timesRepeat: loop equivalent (known iterations) !
10 timesRepeat: [ | i |
    'Count: ', i asString printNl.
    i := i + 1. ].

! to:do: loop equivalent (known iterations) !
1 to: 10 do: [ :i |
    'Count: ', i asString printNl.
].

! Loop with break and continue (using a flag) !
| i breakFlag |
i := 1.
breakFlag := false.
[ i <= 100 ] whileTrue: [
    ( (scoreList at: i) = TARGET_VALUE )
        ifTrue: [
            'Found at index: ', i asString printNl.
            breakFlag := true.
        ].
    breakFlag ifFalse: [
        ( (scoreList at: i) < 0 )
            ifTrue: [ ]   @r{Skip to next iteration}
            ifFalse: [
                'Processing positive value: ', (scoreList at: i) asString printNl.
            ].
    ].
    i := i + 1.
] whileFalse: [ breakFlag ].
@end example

@node Breaking loops
@section Breaking out of loops safely

Early loop exit is achieved using a flag or by returning from a block:

@example
Exit whileTrue: loop early
| i |
i := 1.
[ i <= 100 ] whileTrue: [
    ( (scoreList at: i) = TARGET_VALUE )
        ifTrue: [
            'Found at index: ', i asString printNl.
            ^ self   @r{Exit loop immediately (by returning from enclosing method)}
        ].
    i := i + 1.
].

! Skip current iteration !
| i |
i := 1.
[ i <= 100 ] whileTrue: [
    ( (scoreList at: i) < 0 )
        ifTrue: [ ]   @r{Skip to next iteration}
        ifFalse: [
            'Processing non-negative value: ', (scoreList at: i) asString printNl.
        ].
    i := i + 1.
].

! Nested loop control !
| i j |
i := 1.
[ i <= 10 ] whileTrue: [
    j := 1.
    [ j <= 10 ] whileTrue: [
        ( (matrix at: i) at: j ) = 0
            ifTrue: [ ^ self ]   @r{Exit inner loop only (by returning from enclosing method)}
        ].
    j := j + 1.
].
    i := i + 1.
].
@end example

@node Dialogue
@section Calling a dialogue script (Forth scripts)

SmallTalk does not directly call Forth dialogue scripts. Instead, it uses
its own methods. However, through the semantic-sugar
layer, SmallTalk commands can be mapped to calls of the shared Fountain/Forth
runtime library:

@example
! Original SmallTalk code !
| x |
x := 150.
( (x > 100) and: [ x < 200 ] )
    ifTrue: [ self showMessage: MSG_WELCOME ] .

! Desugared via semantic-sugar layer !
( (x > 100) and: [ x < 200] )
    ifTrue: [ (:call-acc :target "ShowWelcomeMessage" :using nil) ] .
@end example

@node Tiles
@section Manipulating tiles

Tile manipulation is performed through direct video memory access
or through library routines for tiled backgrounds:

@example
! Direct memory access (mode-dependent) !
| videoMemory offset tileNum attribute |
videoMemory := 16rB8000 asSmallInt.
offset := ((row * 80) + col) * 2.
tileNum := 42.
attribute := 7.
(videoMemory byteAt: offset) put: tileNum.
(videoMemory byteAt: offset + 1) put: attribute.

! Library routine approach !
someObject loadTileSet: tileSetId.
someObject placeTile: layer atX: x atY: y tileIndex: tileIndex.
someObject scrollBackground: dx by: dy.
@end example

@node Decals
@section Manipulating decals

Decals (sprites or movable objects) are manipulated using:

@example
! Hardware sprite approach (if available) !
someObject initSprites.
someObject setSpritePosition: spriteId atX: x atY: y.
someObject setSpriteImage: spriteId to: imageId.
someObject showSprite: spriteId withVisibility: -1.   @r{-1 = visible}

! Software sprite approach !
| sprite |
sprite := Dictionary new.
sprite at: #x put: 100.
sprite at: #y put: 50.
sprite at: #frame put: 0.

sprite at: #x put: 100.
sprite at: #y put: 50.
sprite at: #frame put: ((sprite at: #frame) + 1) \\ 4.
@end example

@node Objects
@section Manipulating objects

Object manipulation depends on the implementation approach:

@example
! Using classes !
Object subclass: #GameObject
    instanceVariableNames: 'x y width height active'
    classVariableNames: ''
    poolDictionaries: ''
    category: 'Phantasia-Game' !

GameObject>>initialize
    super initialize.
    x := 0.
    y := 0.
    width := 0.
    height := 0.
    active := true.

GameObject>>moveDX:dx dy:dy
    x := x + dx.
    y := y + dy.

! Create instances !
| gameObjects |
gameObjects := Array new: 20.
1 to: 20 do: [ :i |
    | obj |
    obj := GameObject new.
    obj initialize.
    obj x: 100; y: 100; width: 16; height: 16.
    gameObjects at: i put: obj
].

! Update object position !
gameObjects do: [ :obj |
    obj moveDX: dx dy: dy
].

! Check active status and draw !
gameObjects do: [ :obj |
    obj active ifTrue: [ self drawObject: obj ]
].
@end example

@node Unsupported
@section Unsupported language features

The following SmallTalk features are not supported in this EIGHTBOL release:

@table @asis
@item File I/O operations (FileStream read/write)
@item Graphics commands (Graphics context: display, drawLine, etc.)
@item Hardware-specific features (joystick, mouse, keyboard beyond basic)
@item Extended memory handling (unless emulated)
@item Multithreading or concurrent execution (unless using Semaphore or Monitor)
@item Exception handling (Signal, Responder, etc.)
@item Pointer arithmetic and manual memory management
@item Preprocessor directives (#define, #ifdef)
@item Object-oriented multiple inheritance (unless using traits or mixins)
@item Just-In-Time compilation (unless using external VM)
@end table

